  "prediction": "The report outlines the Public Safety Officers' Benefits (PSOB) and Education Assistance (PSOEA) programs, which provide death and disability benefits, as well as education financial assistance, to eligible public safety officers and their families. The PSOB program offers a lump-sum death benefit of $359,316 and monthly disability benefits to officers who become totally and permanently disabled in the line of duty. The PSOEA program offers monthly education benefits to eligible spouses and children of public safety officers until they reach age 27 or complete their education, whichever comes first.\n\nTo be eligible for PSOB benefits, officers must have sustained their injury or death in the line of duty while employed by a state or local government agency or while performing a qualifying federal duty. The PSOB program also covers certain types of death due to heart attacks and cancer.\n\nThe PSOEA program provides education benefits equal to the monthly benefit amount of the Federal Student Loan Repayment Program, which is currently $1,224 for full-time students. The maximum duration of PSOEA benefits is 30 months for full-time education and 48 months for part-time education.\n\nThe report also discusses the application process for PSOB and PSOEA benefits, including the deadlines for filing claims and the appeal process for denied claims. The PSOB program is funded through mandatory appropriations, while PSOEA benefits are funded through discretionary appropriations.\n\nOverall, the PSOB and PSOEA programs provide crucial financial support to public safety officers and their families in the event of injury, disability, or death in the line of duty. The programs offer valuable benefits that help ensure the financial security and well-being of those who risk their lives to protect our communities.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report outlines the Public Safety Officers' Benefits (PSOB) and Education Assistance (PSOEA) programs, which provide death and disability benefits, as well as education financial assistance, to eligible public safety officers and their families. The PSOB program offers a lump-sum death benefit of $359,316 and monthly disability benefits to officers who become totally and permanently disabled in the line of duty. The PSOEA program offers monthly education benefits to eligible spouses and children of public safety officers until they reach age 27 or complete their education, whichever comes first.\n\nTo be eligible for PSOB benefits, officers must have sustained their injury or death in the line of duty while employed by a state or local government agency or while performing a qualifying federal duty. The PSOB program also covers certain types of death due to heart attacks and cancer.\n\nThe PSOEA program provides education benefits equal to the monthly benefit amount of the Federal Student Loan Repayment Program, which is currently $1,224 for full-time students. The maximum duration of PSOEA benefits is 30 months for full-time education and 48 months for part-time education.\n\nThe report also discusses the application process for PSOB and PSOEA benefits, including the deadlines for filing claims and the appeal process for denied claims. The PSOB program is funded through mandatory appropriations, while PSOEA benefits are funded through discretionary appropriations.\n\nOverall, the PSOB and PSOEA programs provide crucial financial support to public safety officers and their families in the event of injury, disability, or death in the line of duty. The programs offer valuable benefits that help ensure the financial security and well-being of those who risk their lives to protect our communities.",
  "score": 36.43122626779619,
  "answers": [
    "The Public Safety Officers' Benefits (PSOB) program provides cash benefits to federal, state, and local law enforcement officers; firefighters; employees of emergency management agencies; and members of emergency medical services agencies who are killed or permanently and totally disabled as the result of personal injuries sustained in the line of duty. The Public Safety Officers' Educational Assistance (PSOEA) program, a component of the PSOB program, provides higher-education assistance to the children and spouses of public safety officers killed or permanently disabled in the line of duty. The PSOB and PSOEA programs are administered by the Department of Justice (DOJ), Bureau of Justice Assistance (BJA). However, claimants dissatisfied with denials of benefits may pursue administrative appeals within DOJ and may seek judicial review before the United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit. Each year, Congress appropriates funding for PSOB death benefits, which is considered mandatory spending, and for PSOB disability benefits and PSOEA benefits, which is subject to annual appropriations. For FY2019, the one-time lump-sum PSOB death and disability benefit is $359,316 and the PSOEA monthly benefit for a student attending an educational institution full-time is $1,224. In FY2017, the DOJ approved 399 claims for PSOB death benefits, 82 claims for PSOB disability benefits, and 601 claims for PSOEA benefits."
  ],
